WHAT MATHEMATICS ACTUALLY IS
Mathematics is not just calculation.
It is inventing precise statements, deciding whether they are true,
and writing arguments that force other people (or aliens) to agree.
A statement is true only if it can be shown to hold always.

The whole book trains you to move from “it looks true”
to “here is a written argument that it must be true.”

NUMBER TYPES (SETS OF NUMBERS)
N   naturals     {0, 1, 2, 3, ...}          counting / induction
Z   integers     {..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...}
Q   rationals    { p/q | p,q in Z, q != 0 }
R   reals        all limits of rationals    (includes sqrt(2), pi, e)
C   complex      a + bi                     a,b in R

COMMON VARIABLES
n, m, k, i, j     usually elements of N or Z
x, y, z           usually elements of R
p, q              primes or rationals
f, g, h           functions
A, B, S, T        sets
P(n)              a statement that depends on n
epsilon, delta    small positive reals (analysis later)

SET FUNDAMENTALS
x ∈ A             x is an element of A
A ⊆ B             A is a subset of B
A = B             same elements
∅                 empty set
A ∪ B             union        (or)
A ∩ C             intersection (and)
A \ B             difference
P(A)              power set    (all subsets of A)
|A|               cardinality  (size)

BASIC LOGICAL CONNECTIVES
P ∧ Q             and
P ∨ Q             or
¬P                not
P → Q             if P then Q   (implies)
P ↔ Q             if and only if
∀x                for all x
∃x                there exists x

CORE PROOF TECHNIQUES
Direct            assume P, derive Q
Contradiction     assume P and ¬Q, reach absurdity
Contrapositive    prove ¬Q → ¬P instead of P → Q
Induction         prove P(0) or P(1), then P(k) → P(k+1)
Cases             split into exhaustive situations
Construction      exhibit the object that must exist

